# What GitHub should count as akkar, and what it should not.
#
# The language bar was reading Lua 89%, Shell 7.5%, C 1.3%, Dockerfile 0.9%,
# Teal 0.8%, Go 0.3%, Python 0.2% -- which describes the repository and
# misdescribes the project. Two of those entries are measurement apparatus for
# OTHER frameworks, and the Lua figure silently included five thousand lines of
# somebody else's library.
#
# Every rule below is a factual claim about who wrote the code, not an attempt
# to flatter the chart. Nothing is hidden that akkar is responsible for.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- vendored

# lua-http's HTTP/1.1 half, copied in and modified. Ours to maintain, NOT ours
# to claim: it is 5,488 lines by daurnimator and contributors, MIT, and
# counting it as akkar's own Lua overstates the project by about a third.
#
# `docs/substrate/` and the header of each file record what was taken, what was
# cut and what was changed.
akkar/vendor/** linguist-vendored

# The Gin and FastAPI services exist so `bench/compare/` can measure akkar
# against them. They are fixtures -- deliberately written to answer the same
# routes with the same bytes -- and they are Go and Python because the
# frameworks being compared are.
#
# `bench/compare/METHOD.md` rule 1 is why they exist at all: every candidate
# must do the same work, proven by diffing real responses.
bench/compare/gin/**     linguist-vendored
bench/compare/fastapi/** linguist-vendored
bench/study/apps/*.go    linguist-vendored
bench/study/apps/*.py    linguist-vendored

# ------------------------------------------------------------ documentation

# Type declarations, consumed by `tl check` and by editors. They describe the
# API rather than implement it, and `spec/teal_spec.lua` treats them as an
# assertion about the docs rather than as code that runs.
types/*.d.tl        linguist-documentation

# Teaching examples, including one that is deliberately wrong so the type
# checker has something to catch.
examples/**         linguist-documentation

# ----------------------------------------------------------------- counted

# NOT marked, on purpose, because akkar is responsible for them:
#
#   src/akkar_pq.c    the Postgres driver. C is a real part of this project's
#                     answer -- "C where the mechanical work is, Lua
#                     everywhere else" -- and hiding it would misdescribe the
#                     architecture rather than tidy it.
#
#   bench/**/*.sh     the measurement harness. Written here, and the reason
#                     the numbers in `bench/` mean anything.
#
#   Dockerfile*       real deployment artefacts, tested by `docs/DEPLOY.md`.
